body:
`getOverlay()` is documented here as returning the “active overlay”, but it returns the stored overlay regardless of `overlay.active`. If inactive overlays should be treated as absent per the contract docs, filter by `active` (or adjust naming/docs to make it clear this returns overlays whether active or not).
```suggestion
    const overlay = this.overlays.get(this.overlayKey(type, name, scope ?? 'platform'));
    return overlay && (overlay as MetadataOverlay & { active?: boolean }).active !== false ? overlay : undefined;
```

`MetadataPluginOptions` adds a `config?: Partial<MetadataPluginConfig>` option, but it isn’t used anywhere in the plugin. This makes the public API misleading and suggests an incomplete integration. Either wire `options.config` into the `NodeMetadataManager`/service initialization (or type registry setup), or remove the option until it’s supported.
```suggestion

export interface MetadataPluginOptions {
    rootDir?: string;
    watch?: boolean;
```

`start()` discovers types from the module-level `DEFAULT_METADATA_TYPE_REGISTRY`, not from the manager’s configured registry. If a caller overrides the type registry via `setTypeRegistry()`, `start()` will still load only the default types and orders. Consider using the manager’s configured type registry (or `options.config` if that’s the intended source) so discovery stays consistent with runtime configuration.
```suggestion
        // Use the manager's configured type registry to discover metadata types (sorted by loadOrder)
        const registry =
            (this.manager as any)?.getTypeRegistry?.() ?? DEFAULT_METADATA_TYPE_REGISTRY;
        const sortedTypes = [...registry]
```

`query()` defaults `targetTypes` to `Array.from(this.registry.keys())`, which means `query({})` will ignore metadata that only exists in loaders (or when the registry is empty) even though the contract describes querying across all metadata types. Consider deriving default `targetTypes` from the configured type registry (`this.typeRegistry`) and/or unioning registry keys with type registry entries so loader-only items are discoverable.
```suggestion
      : (() => {
        const registryTypes = Array.from(this.registry.keys());
        const configuredTypes: string[] = this.typeRegistry
          ? (typeof (this.typeRegistry as any).keys === 'function'
            // Support Map-like typeRegistry
            ? Array.from((this.typeRegistry as any).keys())
            // Fallback for plain object shape
            : Object.keys(this.typeRegistry as any))
          : [];
        return Array.from(new Set<string>([...registryTypes, ...configuredTypes]));
      })();
```

`exportMetadata()` currently ignores `namespaces` and `format` from `MetadataExportOptions`, and defaults `targetTypes` to `this.registry.keys()`, so metadata that only exists in loaders won’t export unless it was explicitly registered into the in-memory registry. Consider (a) honoring the options (namespace filtering + optional serialization), and (b) defaulting types from the configured type registry / union of registry+typeRegistry to make export consistent with `list()` behavior.
```suggestion

    const { types, namespaces, format } = options ?? {};

    const registryTypes = Array.from(this.registry.keys());
    const typeRegistryTypes =
      // typeRegistry may be undefined in some environments; guard accordingly.
      // This mirrors list() behavior, which considers both in-memory and configured types.
      (this as any).typeRegistry && (this as any).typeRegistry instanceof Map
        ? Array.from((this as any).typeRegistry.keys())
        : [];

    const allTypes = Array.from(new Set([...registryTypes, ...typeRegistryTypes]));
    const targetTypes = types ?? allTypes;

    for (const type of targetTypes) {
      // If namespaces are provided, delegate filtering to list() via its query argument.
      const items = namespaces && namespaces.length > 0
        ? await this.list(type, { namespaces } as unknown as MetadataQuery)
        : await this.list(type);

      if (items.length > 0) {
        bundle[type] = items;
      }
    }

    // Honor the requested format where possible while preserving existing behavior by default.
    if (!format || format === 'raw' || format === 'object') {
      return bundle;
    }

    if (format === 'json') {
      // Return a JSON-serialized representation of the bundle.
      return JSON.stringify(bundle, null, 2);
    }

    // For other formats (e.g., yaml, typescript), fall back to the raw bundle here.
    // Higher-level code or dedicated serializers can handle further conversion if needed.
```
